 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-021\", \"clauses\": {\"describe_position\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"The {objects_plural} are placed in a row. One {object_singular} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes its position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 asks the learner to name the ordinal word that describes the position of one object in a row, which is describing the position of objects. n is drawn from 2, 4 to 5, 7 to 8 in observed renders and its domain is a number in the lc's range, and the object nouns come from the generator's fixed object list.\"}, \"objects\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"The {objects_plural} are placed in a row. One {object_singular} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes its position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 places named objects (baskets, eggs, marbles and so on) in a row, so the problem addresses positions of objects.\"}, \"ordinal_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints ordinal number symbols (1st to 10th in its printed list) and asks the learner to relate each to its ordinal word.\"}, \"1st\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints the symbol 1st in its printed list, so a problem can address the 1st ordinal.\"}, \"2nd\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints the symbol 2nd in its printed list, so a problem can address the 2nd ordinal.\"}, \"3rd\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints the symbol 3rd in its printed list, so a problem can address the 3rd ordinal.\"}, \"up_to_10th\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints the symbol 10th in its printed list, so a problem can reach the upper end of the range up to 10th.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F03\", \"F12\", \"F14\", \"F16\", \"F20\", \"F21\", \"F22\", \"F25\", \"F31\", \"F32\", \"F33\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"'{capitalize}' is an {ord_label} word.\", \"The number {n} in order is called the {symbol} ({word}).\", \"The {lineup} are lined up on a shelf from left to right. Which one is in {symbol} place?\", \"The {symbol} ({word}) place is position number {n}.\", \"Who arrived earlier: the student in {symbol} place or {symbol2} place?\", \"Write the symbol for the {word} position.\", \"{capitalize} = position {n}, written as {symbol}.\", \"{n}{v}\", \"{w}\", \"{answer}\", \"{member}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F31 (template '{w}', basis _ORDINAL_WORDS) includes eleventh through twentieth. These are beyond the lc's up to 10th range and outside student_vocab 1st to 10th, and F31 is reachable as a correct answer, a distractor, and through F03 and F22. F25 ('{n}{v}') draws its suffix v from nd, rd, st, th with no derivation from n, so a learner can see a mismatched ordinal such as a 2 with st; it is a correct answer, a distractor, and nested in F20. F20 ('Who arrived earlier: the student in {symbol} place or {symbol2} place?') takes symbol2 from F25, F32 or F33; F32 and F33 print names (Ana, Carlo and others) and objects (egg, marble and others) in the ordinal place slot, so a learner can be asked to compare a place with a name or an object. F32 ('{answer}') and F33 ('{member}') are therefore failing as well. F12, F16 and F22 print a word from the word domain and a symbol or number from n; value_derivations ties symbol to n but ties word to nothing, so the word can contradict the symbol or n in the same sentence. F21 ('Write the symbol for the {word} position.') asks for a word that is not tied to the keyed answer, which is derived from symbol. F03 ('{ord_label}' from F01, which prints the gate word 'order word', followed by the literal word 'word' in the template) renders as a doubled word, which is unreadable for a grade 1 learner. F14 ('The {lineup} are lined up on a shelf from left to right. Which one is in {symbol} place?') takes lineup from F23, which can contain names and objects together; with names it prints 'The' before proper names and places people on a shelf. Conflict noted, not failed: F05 ('Compare the numbers: the smaller ordinal position is earlier.') and F17 ('What ordinal describes position number {n}?') use the bare word ordinal, while F01's vocabulary gate for this lc prints order word instead, yet introduces_vocab includes ordinal number. I did not fail them on this, and the packet's gate and introduces_vocab disagree. F13 ('The pupils stand in a row. {actor} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes {actor}'s position?') has no derivation of its answer from n in the packet, so its answer relies on F31, which is failing.\"}",
